00:09:18.547 --> 00:09:19.380
"What is this thing?"

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.147
And it piques, it piques
your brain, you're like,

00:09:22.147 --> 00:09:24.930
"Wait, this is something different."

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:28.350
And then, in Maine, or just United States,

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.060
any state you go to, it's not really

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:30.893
that much different.

00:09:30.893 --> 00:09:32.070
- Yeah.
- Except Florida.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.339
- Except Florida, and
then you'll get shot.

00:09:34.339 --> 00:09:35.943
(men laughing)
Then die.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.090
But yeah, that's what I like about Japan.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.549
The whole, you have an
umbrella and you put it in

00:09:42.549 --> 00:09:45.300
the slot thing and it
just, and it takes off.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.142
- Oh, you lock the umbrella?
- It has a bag.

00:09:47.142 --> 00:09:48.213
- Oh, the bag thing.
- It's a bag!

00:09:48.213 --> 00:09:49.046
And I was like,

00:09:49.046 --> 00:09:50.622
"I've never seen this."
- The umbrella condom.

00:09:50.622 --> 00:09:51.879
- Yeah!

00:09:51.879 --> 00:09:53.430
I was like, "This is crazy."

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.680
And also the workers in America.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.290
Every time you go like,
let's say 7-Eleven.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.503
First of all, you got a bunch
of druggie guys over there.

00:10:01.503 --> 00:10:03.442
What you call them, homeless
people or something?

00:10:03.442 --> 00:10:05.942
(men chuckle)

00:10:07.213 --> 00:10:08.643
- Yeah.

00:10:08.643 --> 00:10:11.001
I mean, I guess, I dunno.

00:10:11.001 --> 00:10:13.830
I dunno what the politically
correct term for that is, but.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.390
- I'm pretty sure druggie guys is not

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.287
the political correct term.

00:10:19.626 --> 00:10:20.459
- Sketchy guys.

00:10:20.459 --> 00:10:21.676
- Sketchy, yeah.
- Sketchy.

00:10:21.676 --> 00:10:22.509
- Sketchy.
- Sus guys.

00:10:22.509 --> 00:10:23.520
- Especially in New York.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.480
Every time I leave at
3:00 AM in the morning,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:30.930
I'll always get pursued by, you know?

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.010
- Yeah, that sounds terrifying.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:33.207
- Pan handler.
- Yeah.

00:10:33.207 --> 00:10:36.840
- And then they'll follow
you into the store and then,

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.177
and then they, you know, I'm like,

00:10:38.177 --> 00:10:41.040
"Well, I would give you money,
but if I gave you money,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.790
that means I would have to give $1,

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.900
no, I'd have to give 50 bucks every day

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.210
because you literally
get begged that much."

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:49.590
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.680
- So I'm like, I don't
know, it's just nonstop.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:53.640
- Right, right, right.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:56.910
- So anyway, so, the gas station story.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:58.920
Go back to Japan, right?

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.080
Also, by the way, the workers in 7-Eleven

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:02.760
or wherever they are, they look like

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.060
they don't wanna be there.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.113
They look sad.

00:11:06.113 --> 00:11:07.440
- What, in America or Japan?

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:08.273
- In America.

00:11:08.273 --> 00:11:09.437
- Oh, okay.
- I like that though.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.240
- Yeah, I mean, it's pretty
chill 'cause you're just like,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.260
you go in.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.780
- I don't think you're
paid enough to also then

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.850
have to pretend to be happy about it.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.250
- I mean, to be fair, it's
kind of the same way in Japan

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:21.120
as well.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:22.327
- They pretend to be happy, and I'm like,

00:11:22.327 --> 00:11:23.657
"You don't get paid enough."

00:11:24.892 --> 00:11:26.324
But sometimes they're chill though.

00:11:26.324 --> 00:11:27.975
- Yeah, yeah.
- They're sometimes chill.

00:11:27.975 --> 00:11:28.980
- Sometimes they're chill.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:30.090
Yeah, yeah.
- Sometimes.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:30.923
- Well, there was one time.

00:11:30.923 --> 00:11:33.750
Actually, I think this happened
when I just came to Japan.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:34.740
I went to a 7-Eleven.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.420
I went to get a beer.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.450
A Strong Zero or whatever,
and then it wasn't scanning.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:40.283
The guy ran.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:43.080
He ran because it wasn't scanning.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.664
He ran to the fridge, and
then he ran back, like.

00:11:45.664 --> 00:11:47.595
(panting)
(speaks Japanese)

00:11:47.595 --> 00:11:50.206
And I was like, "You
don't need to do that."

00:11:50.206 --> 00:11:51.039
(Garnt laughing)

00:11:51.039 --> 00:11:53.804
- And he's bowing and stuff, apologizing.

00:11:53.804 --> 00:11:55.380
I'm like, "This is so sad."

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.140
I like the service you're
giving me, but I feel bad

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.840
that you have to feel like
you have to act this way.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:01.770
- Yeah.
- But that's the thing

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:02.880
though, right?

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:07.440
Japan's standard for customer
service is so insanely high

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.790
that there is probably some
Japanese guy or girl out there

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:12.697
who would complain, being like,

00:12:12.697 --> 00:12:13.530
"Come on, hurry up."

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:14.820
- Yeah, yeah.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.660
- It makes me sad a little
bit, but at the same time,

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:17.820
I get good service, you know?

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:18.972
- Yeah, exactly.

00:12:18.972 --> 00:12:20.951
(men laugh)

00:12:20.951 --> 00:12:22.800
- It ain't my problem.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.740
- Sometimes the service is
so good that it's imposing.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:29.190
I'm just like, "I don't need the service

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:30.023
to be this good, bro.

00:12:30.023 --> 00:12:31.960
You can chill" You know?

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.900
- Sometimes they do it so
good that next time you go

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:34.890
to the same place.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:36.660
like when you go to a ryokan, right?

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.610
Sometimes when you get
there, they welcome you

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:39.750
like you're a king, dude.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.550
- [Daidus] Yeah, that's what I'm saying.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:43.560
- They take your bags,
they're looking after you,

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.180
and then other times you'll
go to another ryokan,

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.850
you'll be like, "I hope they
do it, but I won't expect it."

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.087
And then you get disappointed
when they're like,

00:12:49.087 --> 00:12:50.566
"All right, well, what do you want?"

00:12:50.566 --> 00:12:51.860
(Garnt laughs)

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.570
- When you do find that
one guy that doesn't care,

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.791
you're just like, "What is this?

00:12:56.791 --> 00:12:57.624
This is rare."

00:12:57.624 --> 00:12:58.457
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:58.457 --> 00:13:00.390
- It completely warps
your perspective on it,

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.940
even though you go
anywhere else in America

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.780
and it's just normal.
- It's normal, yeah.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:11.250
- It's just like, it's
warped my perception of,

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.800
you know, what I find is good service

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:18.030
and what even stands out to
me, 'cause I remember, I was,

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.530
'cause I was in England last week.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.977
I remember just going to a
normal restaurant, you know?

00:13:22.977 --> 00:13:24.870
And it was such a small thing.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.420
Because I was so used to
just the Japanese service

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:30.540
being as polite and as,
you know, soft as they can.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.330
So I remember, it was just,
I went to a restaurant

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.303
and then I asked for the bill, right?

00:13:34.303 --> 00:13:36.510
And it was just such a small thing.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.870
They slammed it down on the
table and said, "It's a."

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:41.860
They slammed down the
table and said, "It's £50."

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:45.539
I was just like, "Well,
that was a bit aggressive."

00:13:45.539 --> 00:13:47.087
You know what I mean?

00:13:48.301 --> 00:13:50.607
It surprised me because I
was so used to, you know,

00:13:50.607 --> 00:13:53.387
Japanese service, just bringing it to you.

00:13:53.387 --> 00:13:55.338
- They'd probably be like,
"£50" (speaks Japanese).

00:13:55.338 --> 00:13:56.670
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly, right?

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:57.503
Exactly, right?

00:13:57.503 --> 00:13:59.070
I didn't even get a please,
you know, or something,

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:00.663
or you know, or thank you.

00:14:01.677 --> 00:14:02.510
"£50."

00:14:03.393 --> 00:14:05.670
- That is quite odd even
for the UK to be fair.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:07.500
- Really?
- Really?

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:08.850
I don't know, 'cause I've,

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:10.440
it's been so long since
I've been to the UK

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.330
that I'm just like, "Am I an
asshole for thinking that was

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.470
a bit aggressive?"

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.600
- Normally they don't say
the price out loud, right?

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.749
Am I crazy?

00:14:16.749 --> 00:14:18.237
- Oh, they kind of just
like, "Here's the bill."

00:14:18.237 --> 00:14:19.438
- They just give it to you.

00:14:19.438 --> 00:14:21.480
- What's interesting, they
bring the bill to you in the UK,

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:24.360
but in Japan I noticed
you always have to go up.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:25.193
- It depends on the restaurant.

00:14:25.193 --> 00:14:26.053
- Oh, really?

00:14:26.053 --> 00:14:29.220
I haven't been to a single one
where they gave you the bill.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:31.020
- Yeah, for some reason,
sometimes you have to pay

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.790
at the table, so they bring you the bill.

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:33.950
- Normally the fancier it is

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:35.730
or the more European it's trying to be,

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:36.759
you pay at the table.

00:14:36.759 --> 00:14:37.592
- Yeah, you pay at the table.

00:14:37.592 --> 00:14:38.425
- So then if it's just like-

00:14:38.425 --> 00:14:39.514
- But if it's a Japanese
chain, then you can pay-

00:14:39.514 --> 00:14:41.461
- I'm waiting for that moment, bro.

00:14:41.461 --> 00:14:42.294
- Really?

00:14:42.294 --> 00:14:43.140
- Yeah, hasn't happened yet.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:44.642
- I'll take you to a place.

00:14:44.642 --> 00:14:45.475
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:14:45.475 --> 00:14:46.308
I know a place.

00:14:46.308 --> 00:14:48.600
- I know a place.
- I know a place.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.280
Best Italian food in Japan.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:54.240
- Yeah, normally if it's
Japanese food, then you'll pay

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:57.570
at the till, but if you go
to a more westernized place,

00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:02.070
then they try to, I guess
embrace foreign culture.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.090
- It's fine 'cause some of the restaurants

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:04.117
will actually have like a thing that says,

00:15:04.117 --> 00:15:05.100
"Pay at the table."

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:06.162
- Yeah.

00:15:06.162 --> 00:15:07.920
- Well, if they want to
embrace foreign culture,

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.300
they should do tips.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:10.903
I'm just kidding. (chuckles)

00:15:10.903 --> 00:15:11.853
- I wish they did.

00:15:11.853 --> 00:15:12.753
- Bro, honestly-

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:14.763
- You don't want tips. (chuckles)

00:15:14.763 --> 00:15:16.740
- No, but how good the
service in Japan is,

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:18.327
sometimes I wish I could-

00:15:18.327 --> 00:15:19.160
- I don't want American tips.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:21.180
I don't want 20% of the fucking bill tips.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:23.442
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Hold the fucking phone.

00:15:23.442 --> 00:15:24.893
- That's why we don't eat out as much.

00:15:24.893 --> 00:15:25.726
- [Connor] I don't blame you.

00:15:25.726 --> 00:15:27.540
- I noticed the grocery store,
if you live in the city,

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.390
the grocery store is more
expensive than eating out,

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:31.223
which is like...

00:15:31.223 --> 00:15:33.060
- Yeah, Japan is one of the rare countries

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.890
where eating out's actually cheaper.

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:35.723
- It's so weird.

00:15:35.723 --> 00:15:37.264
- Last time we said that, we
got some people who were like,

00:15:37.264 --> 00:15:38.097
"How dare you say that?

00:15:38.097 --> 00:15:39.437
It's not true at all."

00:15:39.437 --> 00:15:40.590
- [Daidus] Well, if you
live in a city, I guess.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:41.610
- Certain things are cheaper,

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:43.050
certain things are more expensive.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:44.850
Obviously if you go to,
you know, (indistinct)

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:45.683
and get a pasta,

00:15:45.683 --> 00:15:47.520
you get this much for
like, 300 yen, then yeah,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:48.353
you should make your own pasta.

00:15:48.353 --> 00:15:49.500
But that's pasta.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:51.360
Like if you want a ramen,
you can't make your own ramen

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:52.193
from home.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:15:52.193 --> 00:15:53.760
- It's gonna be so many ingredients.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:57.360
- The melons are like $300, why?

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:58.440
- Have you tried one of them?

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.084
- It's like this and it's 300.

00:16:00.084 --> 00:16:02.010
- It's 'cause some guy rubs it for hours.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:02.843
He's just massaging it.

00:16:02.843 --> 00:16:04.080
- What'd you mean, have you tried one?

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:04.913
- He's not even joking.

00:16:04.913 --> 00:16:06.120
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.680
I'm just asking, have you tried it?

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.018
- No, no, no.

00:16:09.018 --> 00:16:10.281
- It's not worth it, it's not worth it.

00:16:10.281 --> 00:16:11.479
- It's not worth it.

00:16:11.479 --> 00:16:12.600
'Cause I actually saw a,

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:13.860
I actually saw a documentary on this,

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:16.971
on why the melons in
Japan are so expensive.

00:16:16.971 --> 00:16:18.222
- [Connor] They do this
with everything though.

00:16:18.222 --> 00:16:20.422
- Yeah, they do, it's a
very Japanese culture thing

00:16:20.422 --> 00:16:22.710
where it's just like, they only
pick the cream of the crop.

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:25.470
Every melon is graded and it's perfect.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:27.240
It's not just the taste.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:28.110
- [Joey] It's the look.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:31.650
- It's the look, it's the
crinkling on the side as well.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.350
That gets grade as well
for look and aesthetic.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.260
And I'm just like, "I do not give a shit."

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.360
I'm just like, "Is it just kind of sweet?"

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:40.590
Okay, that's cool.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:44.100
- It's because in Japan,
giving fruit is the utmost-

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:44.933
- It's a flex, right?

00:16:44.933 --> 00:16:46.991
- Yeah, it's a flex for
gift giving culture.

00:16:46.991 --> 00:16:50.430
- That's like lobsters, they
used to be slavery food, right?

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:51.451
Peasant food?

00:16:51.451 --> 00:16:52.284
- [Joey] Yeah, yeah, it
used to be peasant food.

00:16:52.284 --> 00:16:54.488
- Now they're a delicacy.

00:16:54.488 --> 00:16:55.545
- I don't know if fruit is the same way.

00:16:55.545 --> 00:16:58.657
(all laugh)

00:16:58.657 --> 00:17:01.890
But yeah, 'cause in Japan
gift giving such a huge thing

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:04.470
and giving fruit to
someone is like, "Oh, shit,

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:05.700
you really care for me."

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:06.533
- Yeah.

00:17:06.533 --> 00:17:07.650
- [Joey] So that's why
they make it expensive.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:09.060
- It won't work on us, right?

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.477
- It won't work on us
'cause we're just like,

00:17:10.477 --> 00:17:12.445
"Just give me a fucking
granny Smith and I'm good."

00:17:12.445 --> 00:17:13.650
- Yeah, just go to the
grocery store and buy me

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:14.483
a cheaper grape.

00:17:14.483 --> 00:17:15.426
- Yeah, exactly.

00:17:15.426 --> 00:17:16.740
- I'm like, "You spent how
much on this watermelon?"

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:17.670
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:21.420
'Cause I've noticed that
about, I guess the Japanese

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.640
artisan culture, which I
guess I'm gonna call it,

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.460
which is like, one thing
I can say about Japan

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.920
is that if you're a craftsman,

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.080
you put your fucking heart and soul

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:32.393
into whatever you're making, right?

00:17:32.393 --> 00:17:35.280
I mean, that's why Japan
has such a high standards

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:37.140
for so many things, right?

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:40.050
And you know, you see this
with, for example, you know,

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:42.120
like Japanese tools, like
Japanese knives for example.

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.490
You see documentaries about, you know,

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:48.120
people who have inherited
their fucking grand,

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.574
great, great grandfather's
fucking blacksmith or so it.

00:17:51.574 --> 00:17:53.020
- Fourth generation success.

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:55.500
- Every single one of those
documentaries is the same.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:56.648
